WEBVTT

00:01:29.792 --> 00:01:33.310
<v Roberto Remedios> Well, are we live?

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:51.861
Hello everyone! Welcome to the
WordPress Accessibility Day 2020.

00:01:51.861 --> 00:01:54.042
My name is Roberto Remedios.

00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:57.774
I'm a user experience and
user interface designer.

00:01:59.027 --> 00:02:00.882
I live in San José, Costa Rica.

00:02:01.423 --> 00:02:06.047
I'm also an accessibility advocate.

00:02:06.072 --> 00:02:11.204
I've been working in Latin America
with accessibility

00:02:11.229 --> 00:02:18.053
with different groups of people:
deaf people, blind people, and

00:02:19.432 --> 00:02:23.395
I mean, I love to share
with all you guys tonight.

00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:29.643
I forgot to thanks on the last talk

00:02:30.645 --> 00:02:35.451
to our sponsors, to our organizers, and

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.422
to my moderator, Kevin,

00:02:39.447 --> 00:02:46.289
who is pasting the questions that
you guys had on YouTube into the page.

00:02:46.314 --> 00:02:48.628
Please remember, if you have any questions,

00:02:48.653 --> 00:02:51.443
just put the questions on the YouTube chat.

00:02:54.118 --> 00:02:58.327
Our next speaker is Susanna Laurin,

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:05.134
and she will be talking about accessibility
by default in authoring tools.

00:03:06.865 --> 00:03:12.299
She is Chief Research and
Innovation Officer at Funka.

00:03:14.124 --> 00:03:20.266
She has more than 20 years of
experience working with accessibility

00:03:20.291 --> 00:03:23.148
at the senior management level.

00:03:24.093 --> 00:03:28.449
She is an international expert

00:03:28.473 --> 00:03:35.594
on European Union accessibility
policy and regulations,

00:03:35.618 --> 00:03:40.764
and she has did several
workshops and write some books

00:03:40.789 --> 00:03:44.121
about the specific EN standard.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:49.695
So you have the control right now, Susanna.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:50.996
Thank you for joining us.

00:03:51.863 --> 00:03:53.220
You can share your screen.

00:03:59.076 --> 00:04:01.282
<v Susanna Laurin> Okay. Thank you, Roberto.

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.719
I think I'm now sharing my screen

00:04:03.744 --> 00:04:06.908
and hopefully I also have my microphone on.

00:04:06.932 --> 00:04:08.417
[Laughing]

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:10.687
Is this working?

00:04:13.804 --> 00:04:15.652
You can nod, at least.

00:04:15.677 --> 00:04:16.843
<v Roberto> Yes, it's working.

00:04:16.868 --> 00:04:19.002
<v Susanna> Okay, perfect. Thank you.

00:04:19.026 --> 00:04:21.809
So, good morning, everyone!

00:04:21.834 --> 00:04:24.202
I'm doing this from Sweden, Europe,

00:04:24.227 --> 00:04:26.778
so it's very early Saturday morning

00:04:26.803 --> 00:04:31.164
and I want to tell you straight
away that I'm not a developer.

00:04:31.189 --> 00:04:34.922
So if you have technical
questions around my presentation

00:04:34.947 --> 00:04:37.863
I would be happy to forward
them to our developers.

00:04:37.888 --> 00:04:40.957
I can't get them to wake up
this early in the morning on a Saturday,

00:04:40.982 --> 00:04:46.105
So unfortunately you will
have to live just with me.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:51.388
But thank you for having me on
this marathon accessibility event.

00:04:51.651 --> 00:04:53.650
It's exciting.

00:04:53.674 --> 00:04:57.357
So, I can't make this work. Why?

00:04:57.588 --> 00:04:59.563
Yeah, so just a little bit about me.

00:04:59.588 --> 00:05:02.755
I am the Chief Research and
Innovation Officer at Funka.

00:05:03.082 --> 00:05:07.101
I'm also the representative
of the UN initiative G3ict

00:05:07.126 --> 00:05:10.399
and the International Association
of Accessibility Professionals

00:05:10.423 --> 00:05:11.654
representative to the EU.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.085
I do a lot of strategic
consulting these days,

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.702
mainly for the European Union, but also for

00:05:19.222 --> 00:05:21.451
EU member states national governments

00:05:21.475 --> 00:05:27.143
when they are transposing
the EU legislation into national law.

00:05:27.721 --> 00:05:30.373
I'm leading the WADEX subgroup,

00:05:30.398 --> 00:05:31.837
which is an expert group

00:05:32.294 --> 00:05:34.308
helping the Commission
and the member states

00:05:34.994 --> 00:05:37.199
implementing the Web
Accessibility Directive,

00:05:37.224 --> 00:05:40.103
one of the recent
legislations in this area.

00:05:40.445 --> 00:05:45.723
and I'm also one of the technical experts
in the ETSI Special Task Force 536.

00:05:46.298 --> 00:05:50.998
and we have been responsible for
updating and harmonizing the EN standard,

00:05:51.412 --> 00:05:55.512
which is the minimum
requirements of the legislation in Europe.

00:05:59.128 --> 00:06:02.746
Just a couple of words on
the company I work for—Funka.

00:06:02.771 --> 00:06:05.082
We are specialists in accessibility,

00:06:05.333 --> 00:06:09.720
but we were founded by the
disability movement in Sweden, and we

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.896
we started as an NGO, really,

00:06:13.921 --> 00:06:16.882
but we converted into a
private company in the year 2000.

00:06:17.286 --> 00:06:19.584
We have our headquarters in Stockholm

00:06:19.609 --> 00:06:24.119
and offices also in Oslo,
and Madrid, and Brussels.

00:06:24.144 --> 00:06:28.817
We do consulting and development
on accessibility and usability.

00:06:28.842 --> 00:06:30.743
We also do a lot of research and innovation

00:06:30.768 --> 00:06:34.482
and that's nowadays the
department that I lead,

00:06:35.023 --> 00:06:39.287
and so we do national projects and
also European and global projects

00:06:39.312 --> 00:06:40.895
in research and innovation.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:45.532
We also cover policy and different
kinds of studies and investigations,

00:06:45.993 --> 00:06:49.969
and we are, of course, also
engaged in standardization,

00:06:49.994 --> 00:06:52.661
which is extremely important
if you want to do accessibility.

00:06:52.989 --> 00:06:54.942
Also, one of the proud founders of the

00:06:54.966 --> 00:06:57.544
International Association
of Accessibility Professionals.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.256
So, in Europe, the Web
Accessibility Directive

00:07:02.281 --> 00:07:06.241
is really turning everything
upside down here.

00:07:06.266 --> 00:07:08.431
We have been living in
a world where we have

00:07:08.431 --> 00:07:10.724
more recommendations on accessibility

00:07:10.749 --> 00:07:12.420
than actual regulations,

00:07:12.759 --> 00:07:14.754
and this is now changing.

00:07:14.778 --> 00:07:17.316
We started with a procurement directive
a couple of years ago.

00:07:17.341 --> 00:07:20.838
Now the Web Accessibility Directive
is covering the public sector,

00:07:20.863 --> 00:07:26.185
and in 2022 we will also have
the European Accessibility Act

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:30.416
which will cover products and services
in some other private sectors.

00:07:30.441 --> 00:07:34.280
We are really moving from
recommendations to regulations.

00:07:34.892 --> 00:07:38.811
and it's an interesting
time to be alive, here,

00:07:39.238 --> 00:07:43.539
but we have a couple of problems
when this is happening, of course,

00:07:43.567 --> 00:07:46.923
and one of the biggest
problems that we have

00:07:48.633 --> 00:07:50.828
tried to solve, or looked into, at least,

00:07:50.853 --> 00:07:55.751
is really that accessibility is much
more than a technical issue, of course,

00:07:55.775 --> 00:07:59.400
and around 50 percent of the
accessibility problems or fails

00:07:59.425 --> 00:08:01.636
that we encounter when we do audits,

00:08:01.661 --> 00:08:03.654
they are created by web authors.

00:08:04.004 --> 00:08:07.838
and this is not a surprise,
because many web authors—

00:08:07.863 --> 00:08:10.771
and this is now in public sector mainly,

00:08:10.796 --> 00:08:14.877
most of the web authors are not
professional communication people,

00:08:14.902 --> 00:08:19.530
and the absolute vast majority of them
are not accessibility experts.

00:08:19.555 --> 00:08:23.780
So, of course they do create
content that is not perfectly accessible.

00:08:25.158 --> 00:08:29.876
and this means that we now have
approximately seven million interfaces

00:08:29.901 --> 00:08:33.769
that are supposed to comply
with the new regulations.

00:08:34.165 --> 00:08:36.953
and with seven million interfaces,
we have—

00:08:36.978 --> 00:08:39.452
I don't know, I can't even
count the number of web authors

00:08:39.477 --> 00:08:41.932
that would need training to make this work.

00:08:41.957 --> 00:08:44.985
And, of course, that's good for us,
because we are specializing in this

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:47.587
and we're happy to provide training,
but it just won't scale.

00:08:48.105 --> 00:08:52.447
It's not enough just to put out
manuals and training and, you know,

00:08:52.471 --> 00:08:55.298
trying to make all these web
authors do the right thing,

00:08:55.323 --> 00:08:58.413
because people change their jobs,

00:08:58.438 --> 00:09:03.290
they get on parental leave, or, you know,

00:09:03.315 --> 00:09:04.780
we change people all the time.

00:09:04.805 --> 00:09:07.703
So we just need to do something
other than only training,

00:09:07.728 --> 00:09:09.920
because that is not—will never be enough.

00:09:10.712 --> 00:09:13.702
And that's when the idea came, that:

00:09:13.727 --> 00:09:17.422
what if accessibility could be
built into websites from the start?

00:09:17.447 --> 00:09:20.309
What if the authoring tools
could do something to help, here?

00:09:21.348 --> 00:09:23.462
maybe everything can't be automized,

00:09:23.487 --> 00:09:27.027
but at least authoring tools
should have the possibility

00:09:27.052 --> 00:09:30.495
to give much better
support to web authors?

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:34.032
That was sort of the idea
behind this research project.

00:09:35.264 --> 00:09:38.498
And we managed to lobby for this idea

00:09:38.523 --> 00:09:40.396
and make more people interested in it,

00:09:40.421 --> 00:09:45.963
and after a while the European Commission
came out with a call for proposals

00:09:45.988 --> 00:09:51.200
on trying to see if authoring tools could
actually be a part of the solution here.

00:09:51.946 --> 00:09:57.393
So, the European funding for research,

00:09:57.419 --> 00:09:59.850
and a very common
way of doing this

00:09:59.874 --> 00:10:02.912
is that you first, you
apply for a pilot project

00:10:03.171 --> 00:10:06.462
where you sort of prove
that your idea or thesis is valid

00:10:06.752 --> 00:10:10.640
and that there is something
to keep looking into.

00:10:10.888 --> 00:10:13.682
And then, if you're
successful in your pilot,

00:10:13.707 --> 00:10:17.307
then you can also have the possibility to
make what is called a preparatory action,

00:10:17.332 --> 00:10:20.152
which is where you actually
perform the real research

00:10:20.177 --> 00:10:23.156
or whatever initiative
is needed to be done.

00:10:23.449 --> 00:10:25.740
So it's a two-step process, often.

00:10:27.033 --> 00:10:31.694
And we did get the opportunity to have a…

00:10:31.753 --> 00:10:34.020
to perform a pilot project
a couple of years ago

00:10:34.267 --> 00:10:39.352
where we looked at the 30 most-used tools
in public sector bodies in the EU.

00:10:39.612 --> 00:10:42.534
So we crawled the internet
for public sector bodies,

00:10:42.558 --> 00:10:45.173
and found the 30 most used tools,

00:10:45.198 --> 00:10:46.740
to make sure that we,

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:50.692
the efforts were made on the
most frequently used ones.

00:10:51.557 --> 00:10:53.552
And we did some piloting and testing,

00:10:53.914 --> 00:10:57.150
So, trying to see what happens if you,

00:10:57.150 --> 00:11:00.379
if you're not an expert in accessibility,
and you're procuring this tool,

00:11:00.404 --> 00:11:04.698
and maybe your supplier is also
not an expert in accessibility,

00:11:04.723 --> 00:11:09.697
because that is the normal case — that is
the majority of these situations.

00:11:10.451 --> 00:11:14.643
What happens then?
If you just acquire an authoring tool

00:11:14.668 --> 00:11:17.540
and a website from
somebody that is not an expert,

00:11:18.218 --> 00:11:20.090
how accessible is it then?

00:11:20.115 --> 00:11:23.631
It's like, I know this is really theoretic,
because it's not really possible,

00:11:23.656 --> 00:11:25.743
but the idea is if you just
sort of push the button

00:11:25.768 --> 00:11:28.339
and up comes a website from your package

00:11:28.652 --> 00:11:30.929
then how accessible is it by default?

00:11:30.954 --> 00:11:32.030
What is built in there?

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:34.454
and the answer was: not much.

00:11:35.615 --> 00:11:37.854
But we did a lot of
surveys and prototypes,

00:11:37.879 --> 00:11:43.547
we talked to suppliers and vendors
and tool makers and so on,

00:11:43.572 --> 00:11:46.085
try to figure out how
much is really included

00:11:46.109 --> 00:11:48.873
in the basic standard templates, and so on.

00:11:49.349 --> 00:11:52.293
And we did a lot of analysis
and also user testing, of course.

00:11:52.318 --> 00:11:56.951
User testing both with web authors,
and also end users with disabilities

00:11:56.976 --> 00:12:00.927
to make sure that the results
were valid from both perspectives.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:04.869
We did also worked a lot
with different stakeholders.

00:12:05.567 --> 00:12:10.600
this was before the corona, so we
could make physical workshops in Brussels.

00:12:10.921 --> 00:12:16.296
We had specific workshops with web
authors from the public sector,

00:12:16.296 --> 00:12:18.140
to see: what are the user needs?

00:12:18.165 --> 00:12:21.354
How can the authoring
tools help these web authors

00:12:21.379 --> 00:12:24.548
to make the right thing.
It should be easy to do the right thing

00:12:24.573 --> 00:12:26.050
when you create content.

00:12:26.515 --> 00:12:30.139
And we also discussed with
the end users with disabilities of course,

00:12:30.164 --> 00:12:33.633
which are the most
common problems or fails,

00:12:33.658 --> 00:12:35.960
or what is the biggest barrier to you?

00:12:36.359 --> 00:12:40.383
And also looking into the
back office, so to speak.

00:12:40.408 --> 00:12:44.245
So, If the web author has a disability,

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:49.376
how accessible is the actual interface
that you meet as author?

00:12:49.401 --> 00:12:51.795
So, the back end, so to speak,

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:54.734
the input part of the authoring tools.

00:12:55.256 --> 00:12:59.466
And then we also discussed with the
tool makers and other vendors, suppliers,

00:12:59.491 --> 00:13:01.006
and also standardization bodies.

00:13:01.031 --> 00:13:03.151
So we had a series of
workshops where we,

00:13:03.456 --> 00:13:06.942
where we really collected user
needs and experiences

00:13:06.967 --> 00:13:10.080
from these four different
stakeholder groups.

00:13:11.141 --> 00:13:14.103
And the result from
this pilot was that, well,

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:16.846
there is not much
accessibility by default out there

00:13:16.871 --> 00:13:20.888
in the most-used tools, unfortunately,
but there is big demand,

00:13:20.913 --> 00:13:23.869
and also, we thought that there would be

00:13:24.669 --> 00:13:26.978
well, a good potential to do something.

00:13:27.286 --> 00:13:31.215
If something is not perfect, then
you have the potential to make it better.

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:37.296
So, the result of the pilot was
really a matrix of the most-used tools.

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:42.401
And also we made three sets of
guidelines for web authors,

00:13:42.426 --> 00:13:44.895
or people who are
procuring or looking for a tool,

00:13:45.047 --> 00:13:48.099
and also for end users with disabilities,
and for the industry.

00:13:49.002 --> 00:13:52.299
And really this was then the
starting point for further research.

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:53.910
And the result of this…

00:13:55.761 --> 00:13:57.781
from the open parts—

00:13:57.806 --> 00:13:59.092
not everything is public

00:13:59.116 --> 00:14:02.893
but the public parts of this project
results can be found on our website.

00:14:02.918 --> 00:14:10.880
It's www.funka.com/we4authors.

00:14:10.904 --> 00:14:14.909
So, “we,” and the number 4, and “authors.”

00:14:17.838 --> 00:14:19.922
And then, of course,
the next phase started

00:14:19.946 --> 00:14:25.188
and we were happy enough to get also
the second phase funded by the Commission.

00:14:25.645 --> 00:14:30.903
And we are now a third
into the preparatory action,

00:14:30.928 --> 00:14:32.701
which is called “WE4Authors Cluster.”

00:14:32.727 --> 00:14:35.205
And, here, we work with
a cluster of tool makers,

00:14:35.229 --> 00:14:38.961
because we think we need now to
dig deep into the real code

00:14:38.986 --> 00:14:41.513
and the real potential of this.

00:14:41.978 --> 00:14:44.883
So, based on the user needs that
we found in the pilot,

00:14:45.367 --> 00:14:48.304
we are going to create new features

00:14:48.329 --> 00:14:53.248
that can be adapted into
whatever tool you use.

00:14:53.823 --> 00:14:55.873
We are going to do extensive user testing

00:14:55.898 --> 00:14:59.895
to make sure that the features
that we create are really good—

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:03.092
they're user-friendly,
they are solving the right problems,

00:15:03.117 --> 00:15:05.647
and that the web authors like them.

00:15:05.672 --> 00:15:07.517
Because otherwise they won't be a success.

00:15:08.522 --> 00:15:13.185
We are then going to try to implement
these features in different kinds of tools,

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:17.703
so that we make sure that we can reach
as big part of the market as possible.

00:15:19.287 --> 00:15:22.988
And we are then, when the project is
finished, by July next year,

00:15:23.013 --> 00:15:27.464
we are going to share code if we are
resulting in code, which I hope.

00:15:27.803 --> 00:15:30.307
or, If we can't produce code,

00:15:30.331 --> 00:15:32.928
then at least we will
share the documentation,

00:15:33.256 --> 00:15:37.004
and prototypes, videos,
visits, or whatever it can be

00:15:37.029 --> 00:15:40.539
from the user testing, to
show as inspiration others.

00:15:40.564 --> 00:15:44.551
Because maybe everything
can't just be a snippet of code,

00:15:44.575 --> 00:15:48.317
and then you can just implement it
quickly in whatever tool.

00:15:48.343 --> 00:15:50.278
 Maybe life is not that easy always,

00:15:50.303 --> 00:15:52.920
so we will also describe the feature,

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:56.221
and describe how it works,
and what people liked with it.

00:15:56.246 --> 00:16:02.067
So that then, the rest of the market can
hopefully use those ideas for inspiration

00:16:02.067 --> 00:16:05.820
and also implement these ideas
in their own tools.

00:16:09.412 --> 00:16:14.409
This is really an important part of
all European research, of course:

00:16:14.433 --> 00:16:18.468
that the result is going to be
for the common good.

00:16:18.493 --> 00:16:21.540
So this is not something we
or the partners will keep

00:16:22.751 --> 00:16:23.919
behind closed doors.

00:16:23.943 --> 00:16:26.220
The idea is really
to share it with the community.

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:32.551
And the members of this
cluster right now are Drupal,

00:16:33.422 --> 00:16:37.173
we have Mike Gifford of Open Concept,

00:16:37.198 --> 00:16:39.408
who is representing Drupal, here.

00:16:39.782 --> 00:16:45.797
We have Plone, represented by Timo
Stollenwerk from kitconcept in Germany.

00:16:46.056 --> 00:16:51.333
We have Sitevision, a Swedish
license-based authoring tool.

00:16:51.801 --> 00:16:57.143
We have Joomla, and Brian Teeman
is the representative for Joomla,

00:16:57.168 --> 00:17:01.312
and the foundation of Joomla in
Germany is also part of the project.

00:17:01.598 --> 00:17:05.791
and we have Umbraco,
represented by Sigma in the UK.

00:17:06.668 --> 00:17:08.779
So, these are the cluster members

00:17:08.803 --> 00:17:11.981
who are representing different
kinds of authoring tools.

00:17:12.006 --> 00:17:14.983
and we are, of course,
also working with other tool providers,

00:17:15.657 --> 00:17:18.360
but we are also closely related

00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:20.965
to the International Association
of Accessibility Professionals,

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:25.050
so that we can reach out
to both vendors, and also,

00:17:26.559 --> 00:17:29.988
customers using these tools,
and the end user organizations.

00:17:30.251 --> 00:17:33.756
So, with IAAP we have
a really broad outreach,

00:17:34.378 --> 00:17:38.063
apart from the partners'
own networks, so to speak.

00:17:38.088 --> 00:17:39.704
and we also work closely with ERRIN,

00:17:39.728 --> 00:17:44.510
which is the European Regions
Research and Innovation Network.

00:17:44.535 --> 00:17:47.877
So, in… with this network

00:17:47.901 --> 00:17:52.592
we can also make sure that we
reach a lot of public sector bodies'

00:17:52.817 --> 00:17:54.430
web authors in Europe

00:17:55.047 --> 00:17:59.640
for user testing and also making
sure that the user requirements are,

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:03.420
are met in a really,
relevant way for them.

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:11.758
After the discussions with the stakeholders
and the different user groups,

00:18:11.783 --> 00:18:14.880
and specifically, of
course, the web authors,

00:18:14.905 --> 00:18:19.781
their needs, and also the ideas and
experiences from our cluster members,

00:18:19.806 --> 00:18:23.500
so, to make sure that whatever
we do is sort of relevant,

00:18:23.525 --> 00:18:26.877
and possible to use
also for the tool makers,

00:18:26.902 --> 00:18:34.273
we have—we set up a set of criteria
for how the features would…

00:18:34.772 --> 00:18:39.566
what kind of problems we wanted to solve,

00:18:39.594 --> 00:18:44.304
how frequently this problem
was occurring, and so on.

00:18:44.329 --> 00:18:48.199
And then, based on these criteria,
we selected 10 different features

00:18:48.224 --> 00:18:50.245
that we wanted to try with users.

00:18:50.818 --> 00:18:57.811
So now we are specifically in the position
in the project, where we are…

00:18:57.836 --> 00:18:59.651
we have already selected 10 features,

00:18:59.676 --> 00:19:03.240
and we are now going to
user-test them, starting in October.

00:19:04.352 --> 00:19:08.680
So we are, right now,
finishing or refining the prototypes

00:19:08.705 --> 00:19:13.126
that we are then going to test
in an iterative process, of course,

00:19:13.151 --> 00:19:16.741
inviting users, web authors,
and others interested

00:19:16.766 --> 00:19:20.251
to test the prototypes and
come back to us with feedback,

00:19:20.276 --> 00:19:23.132
so that we can refine them and make them
better and better.

00:19:23.157 --> 00:19:25.947
So, we have until Christmas
to do this, really,

00:19:25.972 --> 00:19:27.550
but we're starting here in October.

00:19:28.586 --> 00:19:31.792
So, the features that we have selected,

00:19:32.292 --> 00:19:37.808
to try and make sure that we can
support users, web authors with,

00:19:38.147 --> 00:19:42.730
is really how to provide alt
text—alternative text—on images.

00:19:42.755 --> 00:19:44.826
So, the very basic thing,

00:19:44.851 --> 00:19:48.328
but we have  seen that this is
solved in different ways,

00:19:48.353 --> 00:19:49.645
in different authoring tools,

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:54.147
and sometimes it's quite hard
to make it in a good way.

00:19:54.171 --> 00:19:57.558
and sometimes it—well, you have
the possibility to add the alt text,

00:19:57.583 --> 00:20:01.135
but you don't get any support
on how to write it, or when to write it,

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:02.665
or when not, and so on.

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:05.433
So the whole idea here is
to provide more support.

00:20:06.129 --> 00:20:09.721
If something can be maybe mandatory,
we will try to make it mandatory.

00:20:09.746 --> 00:20:11.326
Alt texts can't really be mandatory,

00:20:11.350 --> 00:20:14.236
because not all images
need them, and so on,

00:20:14.261 --> 00:20:19.277
but we can also prompt for
the web author to do something,

00:20:19.302 --> 00:20:21.015
so it's really visible. It's really…

00:20:23.819 --> 00:20:26.068
you're sort of pushed
to do the right thing.

00:20:26.093 --> 00:20:27.400
You shouldn't be—

00:20:27.682 --> 00:20:31.779
you shouldn't have to look
for the place where to write the alt text.

00:20:31.804 --> 00:20:36.596
It should be very obvious and sort of
“in the flow” of your authoring process.

00:20:36.988 --> 00:20:43.277
And then, we can also provide
maybe support at different levels,

00:20:43.302 --> 00:20:44.547
or information.

00:20:44.572 --> 00:20:50.005
So we have all these levels
of automation we can do,

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:53.975
we can either build it in so that it's
really by default, making it accessible,

00:20:54.166 --> 00:20:59.840
or we can prompt it; or we can make
information or support in other ways.

00:20:59.865 --> 00:21:02.058
So we will try different
ways of doing this,

00:21:02.083 --> 00:21:05.491
and see what the
web authors find most useful,

00:21:05.516 --> 00:21:08.403
and then present that to the market,

00:21:08.428 --> 00:21:10.343
when we have a good decision there.

00:21:10.908 --> 00:21:12.757
So alt text is the first one,

00:21:12.783 --> 00:21:18.341
and then we are also prototyping now the
possibility to change language

00:21:18.365 --> 00:21:22.320
when you have multiple
languages on a website or page,

00:21:23.736 --> 00:21:25.963
which is very common in
the European context.

00:21:25.987 --> 00:21:30.533
We often have websites
with more than one language.

00:21:31.085 --> 00:21:34.804
Also, we're looking at documentation
of the accessibility features,

00:21:34.829 --> 00:21:38.559
because our research has shown that
many web authors don't really know

00:21:38.584 --> 00:21:40.298
what their authoring tool can do.

00:21:40.614 --> 00:21:43.089
So how can we make
the documentation better,

00:21:43.113 --> 00:21:45.020
and also, how can we make it,

00:21:45.045 --> 00:21:47.995
or we want to try, if it's better
to have the documentation,

00:21:48.019 --> 00:21:51.031
that information, that
support and the research,

00:21:51.056 --> 00:21:52.069
sort of “in context.”

00:21:52.094 --> 00:21:55.472
So where you are actually doing something,

00:21:55.497 --> 00:21:57.575
that is where you can
find the information.

00:21:57.600 --> 00:21:59.750
Or, if that may be disturbing for you,

00:21:59.774 --> 00:22:03.976
because the user experience
will then be too crowded

00:22:04.002 --> 00:22:06.142
or too overwhelming
with all this information.

00:22:06.166 --> 00:22:10.394
So, maybe it's better to have it sort of on
the side, that you need to go look for it.

00:22:10.419 --> 00:22:13.103
and we have seen
different situations,

00:22:13.127 --> 00:22:16.368
different contexts, and also
different Web author groups

00:22:16.393 --> 00:22:18.413
who prefer one or the other,

00:22:18.438 --> 00:22:21.339
so this will be really interesting
to do A/B testing on.

00:22:22.855 --> 00:22:26.843
Of course, some of the really
big troublemakers in authoring

00:22:27.258 --> 00:22:28.501
is tables and forms,

00:22:28.525 --> 00:22:34.404
so we are going to test
supportive tables creators

00:22:34.429 --> 00:22:36.655
and also forms editors in different way

00:22:36.679 --> 00:22:37.923
that can…

00:22:38.328 --> 00:22:41.216
well, a combination of making it
accessible by default

00:22:41.241 --> 00:22:44.409
and supporting when you do
more complex tables and forms.

00:22:44.434 --> 00:22:46.232
Of course, everything can't be automated,

00:22:46.876 --> 00:22:48.180
or at least not now,

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:54.837
maybe in the future, but we want to make
sure that the author has better support,

00:22:55.881 --> 00:22:56.985
in doing the right thing.

00:22:57.408 --> 00:23:03.130
When it comes to video,
we are testing supporting the editor

00:23:03.155 --> 00:23:07.174
on how to make sure that the video
is compliant with the regulations.

00:23:08.221 --> 00:23:12.460
And then we have four features that
are sort of connected to each other,

00:23:12.484 --> 00:23:14.267
and they all have to do with testing.

00:23:14.292 --> 00:23:19.919
So we want to try out
how best to test accessibility

00:23:19.944 --> 00:23:26.334
while or when you are editing
or publishing something.

00:23:26.359 --> 00:23:32.239
So, we are trying to have
testing procedures built into the editor,

00:23:32.264 --> 00:23:35.577
so while you're actually
creating the content.

00:23:35.602 --> 00:23:39.571
And then another specific sort of easy win

00:23:39.597 --> 00:23:46.227
is if we support web authors in
providing accessible documents,

00:23:46.251 --> 00:23:48.285
specifically PDF documents.

00:23:49.165 --> 00:23:54.936
which is all over the world,
I think, we have inaccessible documents,

00:23:54.961 --> 00:23:58.723
and that is a big issue that
many web authors tell us

00:23:58.747 --> 00:24:00.341
they would like to have support in.

00:24:00.366 --> 00:24:04.812
So, some kind of testing before you, or
while you're uploading your documents,

00:24:04.837 --> 00:24:07.338
so that you know if
the document is okay or not.

00:24:08.191 --> 00:24:11.460
Then we want to test the full page.
So when you have

00:24:13.427 --> 00:24:15.453
provided all the images, and the objects,

00:24:15.477 --> 00:24:18.840
and the content that you
are going to publish,

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:22.217
then, during this content creation phase,

00:24:22.242 --> 00:24:25.751
you could also test all the
different parts and objects

00:24:25.776 --> 00:24:29.625
 that you are going to publish.

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:32.695
So before publishing,
just check if it's accessible or not,

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:36.887
and then, of course, helping you to
remediate this will be an important part.

00:24:37.467 --> 00:24:40.328
And maybe not specifically
for the Web author,

00:24:40.356 --> 00:24:41.857
but for the website owner,

00:24:41.881 --> 00:24:46.687
or the person who is responsible
for the whole accessibility,

00:24:48.329 --> 00:24:50.178
the accessibility of the whole website,

00:24:50.203 --> 00:24:54.431
then, of course there could
also be a built-in testing tool

00:24:54.455 --> 00:24:55.818
for the whole website.

00:24:56.270 --> 00:25:00.429
and please note that we are not
building a new automatic tools

00:25:00.454 --> 00:25:01.683
for accessibility testing.

00:25:01.707 --> 00:25:04.025
There are many out there and
some of them are really good.

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:06.778
So we are not going to
test if they are good or bad.

00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:10.689
It's just: how can they
be combined or included,

00:25:10.689 --> 00:25:13.440
or implemented in the authoring tool

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:16.112
or in the content creation phase

00:25:16.137 --> 00:25:20.908
so that it supports the
web authors in a better way?

00:25:20.933 --> 00:25:25.488
Because what we see is that many
of the testing tools are quite technical.

00:25:25.513 --> 00:25:27.817
And they may be very
helpful for developers,

00:25:28.221 --> 00:25:31.078
but the web authors are
sometimes not very technical,

00:25:31.103 --> 00:25:32.520
so it can't be too…

00:25:34.166 --> 00:25:38.642
sort of success-criteria-related and
really technical in the way it's presented.

00:25:38.667 --> 00:25:41.487
It needs to support you in another way.

00:25:41.511 --> 00:25:46.132
and we think combining it
into the content creation phase

00:25:46.157 --> 00:25:49.659
could be really helpful for at least
some of the web authors.

00:25:50.311 --> 00:25:53.708
So these are the 10 features
that we are now prototyping

00:25:53.733 --> 00:25:55.016
and are going to test.

00:25:55.041 --> 00:25:58.395
and these are going to be tests,
like generic tests,

00:25:58.717 --> 00:26:02.732
so we have made them
anonymous, so to speak.

00:26:02.757 --> 00:26:05.266
So, we are trying to
just test the feature it,

00:26:05.291 --> 00:26:08.924
then the prototypes are not supposed
to look like any specific tool, but

00:26:08.948 --> 00:26:10.343
but…

00:26:11.959 --> 00:26:17.233
it shouldn't be important
which tool you're used to use.

00:26:17.258 --> 00:26:22.515
but you should be recognizing
the content-creation…

00:26:23.883 --> 00:26:27.453
how it looks when you edit
content. That is the idea.

00:26:27.604 --> 00:26:33.011
So, we're doing this testing on a prototype
level, and then, when we have learned

00:26:33.036 --> 00:26:36.955
more or less what web authors need or want,

00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:41.549
then, we're also going to do test
implementations in the cluster members.

00:26:41.575 --> 00:26:46.115
So all the authoring tools
that are working with us in this project

00:26:46.139 --> 00:26:50.109
also have the possibility
to test these features

00:26:50.134 --> 00:26:56.836
in their own technical
environments, so to speak.

00:26:57.232 --> 00:27:02.515
Of course it takes time to implement
these things in authoring tools

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:07.575
and maybe we won't be finishing
this before the project is finished,

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:11.330
but at least we want to make sure that
we have done some test implementations,

00:27:11.355 --> 00:27:16.620
so that we can also say that
we know these features work

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:20.156
in at least some of the technical
environments that we have tested,

00:27:20.181 --> 00:27:22.496
because that makes it more probable

00:27:22.521 --> 00:27:26.103
that they will also be possible
to use for other tool makers.

00:27:26.128 --> 00:27:30.600
So the test implementation in the
specific tools is also an important part.

00:27:31.111 --> 00:27:35.414
But what we are doing right now is,
first of all, the generic testing.

00:27:35.439 --> 00:27:40.683
and because of the pandemic,
we will do some face-to-face

00:27:41.873 --> 00:27:44.005
physical testing at our offices,

00:27:44.030 --> 00:27:47.909
but most of the testing, so the
quantitative part of it,

00:27:47.933 --> 00:27:49.063
will be online.

00:27:49.446 --> 00:27:53.966
So it's actually open for anyone
that would like to test these features,

00:27:53.991 --> 00:27:56.365
and we are happy to welcome anyone.

00:27:56.389 --> 00:27:57.582
We'll come back to that.

00:27:57.607 --> 00:28:02.713
So, will this solve all the
accessibility problems of the world?

00:28:02.738 --> 00:28:04.370
No, it won't.

00:28:04.395 --> 00:28:08.632
But we believe that built-in
accessibility by default in authoring tools

00:28:08.657 --> 00:28:12.229
could be an important part of
the solution if we succeed in this.

00:28:13.839 --> 00:28:17.240
Because, I mean, this is
kind of obvious, but if you,

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:20.815
if the authoring tool is supporting you
in your content creation phase,

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:23.153
then you can avoid unnecessary mistakes.

00:28:23.178 --> 00:28:26.060
And this is really the primary goal here.

00:28:27.309 --> 00:28:29.647
Accessibility by default can make it…

00:28:30.123 --> 00:28:32.558
We can start with the hygiene factors that

00:28:32.582 --> 00:28:35.558
the authoring tools shouldn't
create accessibility problems,

00:28:35.583 --> 00:28:39.557
but it should also support the
author in not doing the mistakes.

00:28:40.776 --> 00:28:43.699
Another important part here
is really to help the non-experts

00:28:43.724 --> 00:28:45.171
to get it right from the start.

00:28:45.411 --> 00:28:47.091
Because, as I mentioned before,

00:28:47.116 --> 00:28:53.283
most of the web authors in public sector
are not specializing in communication,

00:28:53.308 --> 00:28:55.512
and definitely not
specializing in accessibility.

00:28:56.891 --> 00:29:02.087
So we also hope that this can mean that the
governments can create framework contracts.

00:29:02.312 --> 00:29:06.960
So, in the centralized procurement,
that is very used—much in use

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:09.362
in some of the European member states,

00:29:09.823 --> 00:29:16.364
which can mean that tool makers
that do provide accessibility by default

00:29:16.389 --> 00:29:18.997
using our features or other services

00:29:19.022 --> 00:29:24.964
but in some way can prove that they
do provide good support for web authors,

00:29:25.296 --> 00:29:29.744
they can then have an advantage
in the competition

00:29:29.769 --> 00:29:35.184
by being, sort of, checked on beforehand
by the central procurement system.

00:29:35.771 --> 00:29:38.425
So that is what we're hoping.
That a group of tools

00:29:38.450 --> 00:29:40.204
will be, sort of, the front runners here,

00:29:40.229 --> 00:29:43.549
and they will then have the
possibility to easier, at least

00:29:43.574 --> 00:29:45.600
sell their services to the public sector.

00:29:48.111 --> 00:29:54.828
And with that, experts in accessibility,
and also experts in tool making can,

00:29:54.853 --> 00:29:59.052
and the web authors themselves
can focus on more interesting things

00:29:59.076 --> 00:30:01.969
or more complex issues, and sort
of, solving the rest of the problems

00:30:01.994 --> 00:30:04.062
that we can't solve in this problem.

00:30:04.359 --> 00:30:08.166
But that is the aim of this project.

00:30:09.816 --> 00:30:14.353
So, I would like to invite
you all to make contact us.

00:30:14.378 --> 00:30:19.103
and if you are like to—or if one of
your clients or customers would like to try

00:30:19.128 --> 00:30:21.723
if you have any web authors,
if you know web authors

00:30:21.748 --> 00:30:24.515
that would like—are interested
in trying out these features,

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:26.664
then please do contact us.

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:30.515
There is room for everyone
who wants to contribute to this.

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:32.477
And of course, the more
test persons we have,

00:30:32.502 --> 00:30:34.965
from most parts of the world

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:37.004
and with different backgrounds and so on,

00:30:37.028 --> 00:30:38.095
the better it will be.

00:30:38.641 --> 00:30:42.996
So the results will really depend
on the users who want to test.

00:30:43.021 --> 00:30:46.765
We already have
quite a lot of testers.

00:30:47.193 --> 00:30:52.769
I think at least over 100 organizations
have said that they would like to

00:30:52.794 --> 00:30:54.036
join the testing phase,

00:30:54.427 --> 00:30:56.771
but we are always welcoming more.

00:30:56.796 --> 00:30:58.317
So please do contact us.

00:30:58.342 --> 00:31:05.823
You can use my email, which is
Susanna—S-U-S-A-double-N-A—at funka.com.

00:31:07.517 --> 00:31:09.412
We also have a project website.

00:31:09.437 --> 00:31:14.983
It's not very active yet because we
don't have so much results yet to show,

00:31:15.008 --> 00:31:18.031
but we will be more active in this website

00:31:18.789 --> 00:31:19.862
soon, I hope.

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:25.064
So you're also welcome to follow
the project on accessibilitycluster.com.

00:31:26.676 --> 00:31:29.154
And with that, I'm open for questions.

00:31:35.761 --> 00:31:40.602
<v Roberto> Well, thank you very much, Susanna.
We don't have a lot of questions.

00:31:40.626 --> 00:31:43.829
We have one main question at this moment.

00:31:44.119 --> 00:31:46.234
It is the next one:

00:31:46.576 --> 00:31:50.946
you mentioned you will
develop new features.

00:31:51.321 --> 00:31:56.544
Do you have examples of what
kind of features are good candidates?

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:01.363
<v Susanna> Well, before we start testing them,

00:32:01.387 --> 00:32:04.013
we don't know which ones
are the best candidates.

00:32:04.038 --> 00:32:08.146
But really it's the test—is the 10
features that I talked about

00:32:08.171 --> 00:32:09.484
and showed the slide.

00:32:09.831 --> 00:32:12.331
These are the features
that we are now testing.

00:32:12.356 --> 00:32:16.080
And if they all turn out not
to work or not to be

00:32:17.179 --> 00:32:18.598
of interest for web authors,

00:32:18.622 --> 00:32:20.574
then we will have to
come up with new ones,

00:32:20.599 --> 00:32:23.965
because we have promised the
European Commission, who is funding this,

00:32:23.989 --> 00:32:31.518
that we are going to promote
or provide at least 10 features

00:32:31.868 --> 00:32:33.872
when the project is ready.

00:32:34.291 --> 00:32:37.355
But as we are just
starting the testing phase,

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:40.720
I can't tell you which one is
the best candidate right now.

00:32:40.745 --> 00:32:42.322
We are testing all 10 features,

00:32:43.145 --> 00:32:48.691
and then hopefully many of them will be
good enough to present to the world.

00:32:48.716 --> 00:32:50.978
And if not, we will have
to make another round

00:32:51.003 --> 00:32:55.319
and create new ones.

00:32:57.200 --> 00:32:58.705
<v Roberto> Cool, thank you very much.

00:32:59.078 --> 00:33:03.530
Well, at this moment
we don't have any other questions.

00:33:03.555 --> 00:33:08.191
Is there anything that you want
to add on these couple of minutes

00:33:08.216 --> 00:33:11.590
that you didn't cover in your presentation?

00:33:11.615 --> 00:33:13.607
You have, like, another five minutes

00:33:13.632 --> 00:33:17.040
if you have anything,
or we can make a break.

00:33:19.667 --> 00:33:23.760
<v Susanna> I think I'm—I hope that
people will connect with us.

00:33:23.785 --> 00:33:27.299
And I'm happy to answer any question
via email or Twitter,

00:33:27.324 --> 00:33:32.702
or just reach out to us and
we will be happy to discuss this more,

00:33:32.727 --> 00:33:35.619
because I think there are
a lot of interesting things,

00:33:35.644 --> 00:33:41.303
and we are really eager to hear also from
WordPress developers, designers, and users.

00:33:41.328 --> 00:33:46.102
And I hope that you will all
be interested in this project

00:33:46.127 --> 00:33:49.696
and also reach out to us to learn more.

00:33:53.465 --> 00:33:58.318
<v Roberto> Awesome. Well, we can give it
a couple of minutes

00:33:58.343 --> 00:34:02.434
and see if there is another
question on YouTube.

00:34:12.883 --> 00:34:15.755
Okay, we have a question.

00:34:16.586 --> 00:34:19.565
Do testers need to be multilingual?

00:34:21.771 --> 00:34:25.831
<v Susanna> Good question, thank you.
No. The tests will be performed in English,

00:34:25.856 --> 00:34:29.308
so you would need to understand
at least the instructions in English,

00:34:29.333 --> 00:34:30.879
because the prototypes are in English.

00:34:30.904 --> 00:34:34.536
But otherwise, we don't have
any requirements on the testers.

00:34:46.754 --> 00:34:47.894
<v Roberto> Thank you, Susanna.

00:34:51.024 --> 00:34:56.340
Anyone—if anyone has another
question—right here.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:06.300
<v Susanna> I think it's in the middle of
the night in many countries.

00:35:06.371 --> 00:35:11.564
<v Roberto> Yes, actually, for me it's past midnight.

00:35:11.589 --> 00:35:14.626
But what's your time on your country?

00:35:14.651 --> 00:35:17.381
<v Susanna> It's now 8:30 in the morning,
but it's Saturday,

00:35:17.406 --> 00:35:19.281
so I don't think many are up

00:35:19.306 --> 00:35:21.792
<v Roberto> Yeah, most of Europe people—

00:35:22.115 --> 00:35:24.611
<v Susanna> Yeah. Still asleep, I think. Yeah.

00:35:24.636 --> 00:35:26.509
<v Roberto> Just waiting for the Saturday.

00:35:26.978 --> 00:35:31.562
But again, thank you very much for
sharing with us and taking your time.

00:35:36.028 --> 00:35:38.410
get early and share with us.

00:35:38.434 --> 00:35:42.952
I think we're gonna finish on this one.

00:35:46.915 --> 00:35:51.918
I think LNN is making a question.

00:35:51.943 --> 00:35:55.260
It says, “How much time does it take?”

00:35:56.583 --> 00:35:58.671
How much time does it taking testing?

00:36:00.102 --> 00:36:01.670
<v Susanna> That's another good question.

00:36:01.694 --> 00:36:04.054
Well, you can spend as much time
as you like, of course,

00:36:04.079 --> 00:36:07.780
 but you can test just one feature,

00:36:07.805 --> 00:36:12.390
and that will take from five
to, I guess, mostly 10 minutes.

00:36:12.732 --> 00:36:16.311
We, of course, hope that
you will test all the features.

00:36:16.336 --> 00:36:19.242
and you can also come back, because we
have this iterative process,

00:36:19.267 --> 00:36:21.442
so then you can test it again
and see the refinements.

00:36:21.467 --> 00:36:24.777
But you can just spend five or 10 minutes
and do one test,

00:36:24.802 --> 00:36:25.922
and we will be happy.

00:36:25.947 --> 00:36:27.670
And if you are really
interested and keen,

00:36:27.695 --> 00:36:30.180
then you can come back
and test all 10 features.

00:36:30.180 --> 00:36:33.221
and that will then take maybe
one and a half hours or so.

00:36:34.086 --> 00:36:36.812
And you can also come back
and do it more times,

00:36:36.837 --> 00:36:40.424
but there's no specific requirement
on time needed.

00:36:40.449 --> 00:36:43.131
So you can just do a short one
and see if it's interesting

00:36:43.156 --> 00:36:45.660
and then, hopefully, you
will stay on and do more tests.

00:36:50.634 --> 00:36:53.672
<v Roberto> And then, we have
another question from Ahmed:

00:36:55.213 --> 00:36:57.506
if you had to name one thing

00:36:57.530 --> 00:37:01.965
that it would be your biggest
achievement in accessibility,

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:02.995
what it will be?

00:37:03.852 --> 00:37:05.829
<v Susanna> My biggest achievement? Oh.

00:37:07.138 --> 00:37:13.159
Well, personally, I think that we
are hiring people with disabilities

00:37:13.184 --> 00:37:14.393
in our company.

00:37:14.418 --> 00:37:17.138
I think that is the biggest
achievement to me personally.

00:37:17.163 --> 00:37:20.782
I believe the key to inclusion is
to have a workplace,

00:37:20.807 --> 00:37:24.977
and really, that we have been able
not only to hire people ourselves,

00:37:25.002 --> 00:37:29.047
but also help other young people with
disabilities to get into the workforce.

00:37:29.508 --> 00:37:32.683
That is what I'm most proud
of in my professional life.

00:37:32.708 --> 00:37:34.765
That is not really accessibility as such,

00:37:34.765 --> 00:37:39.218
but I think that is my sort of
spontaneous answer to that question.

00:37:41.351 --> 00:37:43.117
<v Roberto> Awesome, thank you very much.

00:37:43.421 --> 00:37:48.013
I think LNN also asked,
for the previous question,

00:37:48.038 --> 00:37:52.320
if the time that you described
is also include the training.

00:37:55.149 --> 00:37:58.867
<v Susanna> Well, there's no training
really here, in the,

00:37:58.896 --> 00:38:02.566
I mean, the testing is just
really simple prototypes,

00:38:02.591 --> 00:38:07.116
so you are doing the same
thing as you're normally doing as

00:38:07.141 --> 00:38:08.646
when you're creating content.

00:38:08.672 --> 00:38:12.740
You are writing a text, you are
adding a link, you're adding an image,

00:38:12.765 --> 00:38:14.954
and then you are supposed
to add the alt text and so on.

00:38:14.979 --> 00:38:17.901
So you are performing
this sort of the user scenario

00:38:17.925 --> 00:38:22.772
from the web authoring perspective.

00:38:22.797 --> 00:38:24.741
And then there's a really short instruction

00:38:24.766 --> 00:38:26.987
on what we want you
to do to perform that test.

00:38:27.012 --> 00:38:30.060
So it's not really—I
wouldn't say it's training really.

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:33.527
So it's a short instruction.
Then you perform the test,

00:38:33.552 --> 00:38:36.000
and then, that is screen recorded,

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:38.567
so we can see what you do.
So that is sort of fine,

00:38:38.592 --> 00:38:42.998
but we also always ask you if you have
any comments or suggestions or anything.

00:38:43.023 --> 00:38:45.497
We're really happy if you want
to share with us your thoughts.

00:38:45.522 --> 00:38:48.982
So you also have the possibility to
answer some questions afterward,

00:38:49.006 --> 00:38:50.409
if you would like to.

00:38:50.434 --> 00:38:55.020
But it wouldn't take more than 15
minutes just to do one of the items.

00:38:59.079 --> 00:39:04.200
<v Roberto> Okay, awesome. I think that's
all the questions that we have.

00:39:05.068 --> 00:39:08.125
We're gonna finish the
presentation right here.

00:39:08.533 --> 00:39:10.081
Thank you very much for your time.

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:14.891
Just to the people who is
watching this presentation:

00:39:14.916 --> 00:39:17.251
don't forget to attend our next talk.

00:39:19.149 --> 00:39:23.072
which will be “How to Use ARIA in Forms”

00:39:23.097 --> 00:39:25.200
at 7:00 a.m. UTC,

00:39:25.200 --> 00:39:32.074
presented by Ryan—Rian Rietveld.

00:39:32.099 --> 00:39:37.560
Sorry, my accent. But yeah, thank you
very much, and we'll see you later. Bye!

00:39:37.911 --> 00:39:39.161
<v Susanna> Bye bye!